      <section class="chapter-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">CHAPTER 01</p>
        <h1>GETTING TO KNOW HTML</h1>
        <p>
          Discover the structure and essential features of an HTML document.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section class="section">
        <h2 class="section-title">1.1 Two Types of HTML Elements</h2>
        <p>
          HTML elements can be understood through two common categories:
          block-level elements and inline elements.
        </p>
        <div class="demo">
          <p class="demo-label">LIVE EXAMPLE</p>
          <div class="element-example">
            <div>
              <strong>Block-level elements</strong>
              <p>This paragraph starts on a new line.</p>
              <p>This paragraph occupies another line.</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <strong>Inline elements</strong>
              <p>
                Read <span style="color: #8b5e3c">this highlighted text</span>
                inside the same paragraph.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p class="code-note">
            Block-level elements normally begin on a new line. Inline elements
            flow within the surrounding text.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="section">
        <h2 class="section-title">1.2 HTML Document Structure</h2>
        <p>
          An HTML document has a standard structure that helps browsers
          understand its content.
        </p>
        <div class="demo">
          <p class="demo-label">DOCUMENT STRUCTURE</p>
          <ol class="structure-list">
            <li><strong>DOCTYPE:</strong> Declares the document as HTML5.</li>
            <li><strong>HTML:</strong> The root element of the page.</li>
            <li>
              <strong>Head:</strong> Contains metadata and the page title.
            </li>
            <li><strong>Body:</strong> Contains the visible page content.</li>
          </ol>
          <div class="result">
            <h3>Welcome to BIBLUNE</h3>
            <p>This content belongs to the document body.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="section">
        <h2 class="section-title">1.3 Your First Webpage</h2>
        <p>
          A webpage can display headings, paragraphs, images and links. Here is
          a small bookstore introduction.
        </p>
        <div class="demo">
          <p class="demo-label">FIRST WEBPAGE PREVIEW</p>
          <div class="result">
            <h2>Welcome to BIBLUNE Bookstore</h2>
            <p>A little world of books.</p>
            <img
              class="profile-image"
              src="Images/Moonlit Dark Academia Desk _ Aesthetic Wallpaper.jfif"
              title="A book from our collection"
            />
            <p>Explore stories, knowledge and new adventures.</p>
            <a class="attribute-link" href="#chapter-list">
              Explore Chapters
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="section">
        <h2 class="section-title">1.4 Character Encoding</h2>
        <p>
          Character encoding determines how letters, numbers and symbols are
          represented. UTF-8 supports a wide range of characters.
        </p>
        <div class="demo">
          <p class="demo-label">UTF-8 EXAMPLE</p>

          <div class="result encoding-demo">
            <p>English: Welcome to BIBLUNE!</p>
            <p>Bangla: স্বাগতম, বইয়ের জগতে!</p>
            <p>Symbols: © ® € ★ ✓</p>
          </div>
          <p class="code-note">
            The UTF-8 character encoding is declared in the document head.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="section">
        <h2 class="section-title">1.5 Element Attributes</h2>
        <p>
          Attributes provide additional information about HTML elements. They
          can specify links, image descriptions, dimensions and more.
        </p>
        <div class="demo">
          <p class="demo-label">LIVE ATTRIBUTE EXAMPLES</p>
          <div class="result">
            <p>
              <strong>Link attribute:</strong>
              <a
                href="https://www.wikipedia.org/"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
              >
                Visit Wikipedia
              </a>
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Title attribute:</strong>
              <span title="This is additional information">
                Hover over this text
              </span>
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Image attributes:</strong>
              The book image above uses <em>src</em>, <em>alt</em>,
              <em>title</em>, <em>width</em> and <em>height</em>-related
              styling.
            </p>
          </div>
          <p class="code-note">
            Try hovering over the title example or opening the external link.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="section">
        <h2 class="section-title">1.6 Favorite Icon</h2>
        <p>
          A favicon is a small icon associated with a website. It usually
          appears in the browser tab beside the page title.
        </p>
        <div class="demo">
          <p class="demo-label">FAVICON PREVIEW</p>
          <div class="favicon-preview">
            <span class="favicon-symbol">B</span>
            <span>BIBLUNE | Getting to Know HTML</span>
          </div>
          <p class="code-note">
            The browser tab icon is linked through the document head. Add a
            favicon.ico file beside this HTML file for the real tab icon.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="section">
        <h2 class="section-title">1.7 Comments</h2>
        <p>
          HTML comments help developers explain or organize their code. Browsers
          do not display comments as normal webpage content.
        </p>
        <div class="demo">
          <p class="demo-label">COMMENT EXAMPLE</p>
          <div class="comment-demo">
            <h3>Our Book Collection</h3>
            <p>Discover books for every curious mind.</p>
            <p class="code-note">
              The HTML comment in this section is not visible as page content.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
